      en: "The Laponia Region is a vast UNESCO World Heritage site in Swedish Lapland, covering 9,400 square kilometers, making it one of the largest protected areas in the world. Designated in 1996, it is recognized for both its outstanding natural landscapes and the living cultural heritage of the indigenous Sami people. Laponia encompasses four national parks—Sarek, Padjelanta, Muddus, and Stora Sjöfallet—along with two nature reserves. The geography features extreme contrasts, ranging from the glaciers and alpine peaks of the Sarek massif to the ancient coniferous forests and expansive peatlands of Muddus. For millennia, this area has been the home of the Sami, who continue to practice traditional reindeer herding based on seasonal migrations. The preservation of this nomadic lifestyle in harmony with the pristine wilderness is a core component of its heritage status. Laponia supports rare species such as the arctic fox, brown bear, and golden eagle, while showcasing profound evidence of glacial erosion.",
